AddRecord 'thm:JT_p_UPFP' @('ThesisCommon.IsLiteralTreeSegment','ThesisCommon.IsConsecutiveTreeSegment','ThesisCommon.LiteralChainJamesTreeSpace','ThesisCommon.ConsecutiveJamesTreeSpace','PFP.Theorem_5_4_asPrinted','PFP.Theorem_5_4_consecutive') 'statement only; two segment conventions explicitly distinguished' `
    'For every finite 1<p, the actual completed James-tree space has UPFP. The manuscript finite-chain convention and the cited consecutive-segment convention are registered as separate targets.' `
    'Both actual norms and completions are constructed. The literal printed target is preserved exactly; a separately named consecutive-segment target records the alternative. Neither UPFP assertion nor equivalence of the two norms is assumed or claimed. No unqualified JT_p alias silently selects a convention.'
AddRecord 'lem:complemented-copy-implies-hyperplanes' @('PFP.Lemma_B_1','PFP.lemma_B_1','ThesisCommon.IsIsomorphicToScalarExtension','ThesisCommon.IsIsomorphicToEveryHyperplane','ThesisCommon.IsClosedHyperplane','ThesisCommon.scalarExtension_of_complemented_subspace','ThesisCommon.IsClosedHyperplane.exists_functional','ThesisCommon.scalarKernelEquiv','ThesisCommon.IsIsomorphicToScalarExtension.everyHyperplane') 'complete' `
    'An infinite-dimensional Banach space containing a complemented subspace isomorphic to its scalar extension is itself isomorphic to its scalar extension and to every closed hyperplane.' `
    'Hyperplanes have actual quotient dimension one and are explicitly closed. The proof decomposes along the complemented subspace and absorbs its scalar coordinate. A closed hyperplane is the kernel of an actual nonzero bounded functional; projections along a common transverse vector give bounded equivalences between any two such kernels. Both original conclusions and the original Banach/infinite-dimensional hypotheses are retained.'
AddRecord 'lem:qref-one-dual-and-summands' @('PFP.Lemma_B_2','PFP.lemma_B_2','ThesisCommon.IsQuasiReflexiveOfOrder','ThesisCommon.IsQuasiReflexiveOfOrderOne','ThesisCommon.residuumSpace_normedAddCommGroup','ThesisCommon.residuumSpace_completeSpace','ThesisCommon.residuumDualLinearEquiv','ThesisCommon.IsQuasiReflexiveOfOrder.dual','ThesisCommon.residuumComplementedEquiv') 'complete' `
    'The dual of a quasi-reflexive space of order one has order one. For every closed complementary decomposition of X, its bidual residuum is isomorphic to the product of the two summand residua.' `
    'Restriction from the triple dual to the original dual has the canonical embedding as a section. Its kernel is the annihilator of the canonical bidual image and hence the continuous dual of the actual residuum quotient. This proves preservation of every finite quasi-reflexive order. Actual bidual product maps descend to quotient maps and give continuous inverse equivalences for every pair of closed complementary summands. Order is module rank, so finite dimensionality is established rather than assumed from a bare finrank.'
AddRecord 'prop:qref-primary-dual' @('PFP.Proposition_B_3','PFP.proposition_B_3','ThesisCommon.IsQuasiReflexiveOfOrderOne.bidualSelfEquiv','ThesisCommon.primary_product_summand','ThesisCommon.nonempty_equiv_of_bidual_equiv','ThesisCommon.primary_dual_of_quasi_reflexive_orderOne') 'complete' `
    'A primary quasi-reflexive Banach space of order one has primary dual if both it and its dual are isomorphic to all of their closed hyperplanes.' `
    'Closed projection ranges give an actual product decomposition of the dual. Dualising and absorbing the one-dimensional bidual defect identifies X with the product of the dual summands. Primariness selects one such summand. The checked quotient dimension argument proves its predual summand has order one; actual hyperplane isomorphisms then identify it with X*. Both original hyperplane hypotheses are used. No reflexivity or space-representation conclusion is added as a premise.'
AddRecord 'cor:separable-coeff-zfc' @('PFP.Corollary_4_9','PFP.corollary_4_9','PFP.corollary_4_9_general','PFP.corollary_4_9_concrete','ThesisCommon.lpUncountableSchauderSumEquiv','ThesisCommon.cZeroUncountableSchauderSumEquiv') 'complete' `
    'For separable coefficients with UPFP and an uncountable index set, every symmetric non-ell1 norm-series sum has UPFP. In particular, every finite 1<p lp sum and the c0 sum have UPFP.' `
    'The general assertion follows from the proved uniform clause of Theorem 4.3(ii). Actual arbitrary-index scalar lp and c0 Schauder families are constructed, with symmetric norms and non-ell1 estimates. Explicit coordinate-preserving linear isometries identify their norm-series sums with the actual vector-valued lp and c0 spaces. UPFP transfers through these maps. Completeness and the printed exponent endpoints are retained.'
AddRecord 'lem:scalar-to-vector-support' @('PFP.Lemma_4_14','PFP.lemma_4_14','ThesisCommon.UncountableSchauderSum.scalarisation','ThesisCommon.UncountableSchauderSum.coord_scalarisation','ThesisCommon.UncountableSchauderSum.operator_countable_support_of_scalar') 'complete' `
    'If every bounded map from X into the scalar basis space has a countable common coordinate support, then every map from X into the vector-valued sum also has such a support.' `
    'Solidity constructs an actual contraction from coordinatewise norm-at-most-one functionals into E. Hahn--Banach norms one chosen witness at each active output coordinate. The scalar-support hypothesis for this one constructed operator excludes every active coordinate outside its countable support. This expands the same source scalarisation argument without the unnecessary intermediate uncountable epsilon subselection. The countable set is uniform over the whole operator range.'
AddRecord 'lem:operator-countable-general' @('PFP.Lemma_4_15','PFP.lemma_4_15','PFP.PreservesCountableCoordinateSupports','ThesisCommon.NormalizedSchauderFamily.separable_countable_support','ThesisCommon.UncountableSchauderSum.countable_block_support_of_separable_scalar_ranges') 'complete' `
    'If every bounded map from X to the scalar basis space has separable range, each endomorphism of the vector-valued sum sends each countable-coordinate subspace into another countable-coordinate subspace.' `
    'The dense-set argument gives scalar common supports, and the proved scalarisation gives the vector version. Take the countable union of supports of T composed with each input-coordinate insertion. Continuity and norm convergence of the genuine finite-subset expansions extend the assertion to every vector in the source block.'
AddRecord 'cor:operator-countable' @('PFP.Corollary_4_16','PFP.Corollary_4_16_separable','PFP.Corollary_4_16_ellOne','PFP.corollary_4_16','PFP.corollary_4_16_separable','PFP.corollary_4_16_ellOne','ThesisCommon.isCompact_norm_of_weakScalarEllOne','ThesisCommon.IsWeaklyCompactlyGenerated.ellOne_operator_separable_range','ThesisCommon.IsWeaklyCompactlyGenerated.ellOne_countable_block_support') 'complete' `
    'Countable-coordinate support preservation holds for separable coefficients in general symmetric sums, and for WCG coefficients in ell1 sums.' `
    'Both alternatives are proved. In the WCG branch, each sequence in a weakly compact scalar ell1 set has countably many active coordinates. Coordinates embed the corresponding compact subspace into a countable product; a weakly convergent subsequence and the checked real/complex Schur theorem give norm compactness. The image of the weakly compact generating set therefore has separable closed span. Actual lp Hahn--Banach scalarisation and finite-coordinate norm convergence finish the source argument for arbitrary Gamma.'
AddRecord 'lmm: disjoint-1' @('PFP.Lemma_4_17','PFP.lemma_4_17','ThesisCommon.finite_permutation_into_infinite','ThesisCommon.UncountableSchauderSum.equivalentEllOne_of_large_fibres','ThesisCommon.UncountableSchauderSum.functional_countable_coordinate_support') 'complete' `
    'For a symmetric non-ell1 sum and T into X, each scalar functional on X annihilates T on all but countably many singleton coordinates.' `
    'Infinitely many uniformly large fibre restrictions give unit-ball witnesses. Scalar phases align their values, and finite permutation symmetry transports the resulting ell1 lower estimate to every finite subset of Gamma, contradicting the non-ell1 hypothesis. Thus positive restriction-norm levels are finite; their countable union controls every nonzero restriction. Annihilation is zero composition with the actual bounded singleton projection.'
AddRecord 'lmm: disjoint-2' @('PFP.Lemma_4_18','PFP.lemma_4_18','ThesisCommon.HasCountableTotalDual','ThesisCommon.UncountableSchauderSum.operator_countable_incoming_support') 'complete' `
